{
  "result": "There is a strange comfort in train stations at dawn. The air is still cool, the sky a washed blue, and the speakers crackle with half-understood announcements. People stand in small islands of solitude, clutching coffee, scrolling phones, or simply staring down the tracks as if willing the train to appear faster.\n\nI love watching how travel makes us both more anonymous and more human. No one knows your name on platform three, yet everyone shares the same quiet hope that the train will be on time, that the seats will be empty enough to sprawl. Strangers exchange glances when the wind lifts an abandoned newspaper or when a pigeon lands too close.\n\nMovement itself feels like thinking. The world outside the window blurs into streaks of green and grey, and your mind is finally permitted to wander without purpose. You are not required to be productive when you are in transit. You are allowed to just be between places, which is a rare and underrated state of existence.\n\nWe spend so much of life fixated on arrivals. The job, the degree, the relationship, the destination city. But the in-between is where most living actually happens. The conversation with the elderly woman across the aisle. The sudden smell of rain through an open door. The way light falls across an empty seat.\n\nPerhaps we would all be gentler if we remembered we are always just passing through. And perhaps that is enough to be kind to everyone we meet today.",
